---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BoxSizeCalculator from '../../components/BoxSizeCalculator';
import '../../styles/packmetry-workspace.css';
import '../../styles/box-size-tool.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Box Size Calculator — Packmetry"
  description="Find the minimum internal box dimensions needed for one rectangular item under a chosen rotation policy, or check whether an available box fits geometrically."
>
  <main class="pm-box-size-page">
    <div class="pm-box-size-shell">
      <header class="pm-box-size-hero">
        <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
          Single-item box sizing
        </p>

        <h1>
          Find the internal box size
          one item actually needs.
        </h1>

        <p>
          Enter one rectangular item, choose how it
          may rotate, and see the distinct minimum
          internal box profiles that can contain it.
          If you already have a box, you can check
          that box&apos;s internal dimensions too.
        </p>
      </header>

      <BoxSizeCalculator client:load />

      <div class="pm-box-size-copy">
        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
            What “minimum” means
          </p>

          <h2>
            A geometric minimum, not a packaging
            allowance recommendation.
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each result profile matches one allowed
            orientation of the item. The box needs
            at least those internal dimensions for
            that orientation. Packmetry does not add
            a hidden padding, void-fill or
            manufacturing-tolerance allowance.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
            Rotation matters
          </p>

          <h2>
            The same item can require different
            axis dimensions.
          </h2>

          <p>
            With unrestricted rotation, an item can
            map its original length, width and height
            to different box axes. Upright and
            vertical-axis-only policies keep the
            original height on the vertical axis;
            fixed orientation keeps all three axes
            as entered.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
            Worked example
          </p>

          <h2>
            120 × 80 × 40 mm, kept upright.
          </h2>

          <p class="pm-box-size-example">
            The distinct minimum internal profiles
            are 120 × 80 × 40 mm and
            80 × 120 × 40 mm. The height remains
            40 mm because the item stays upright.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
            Single item only
          </p>

          <h2>
            Multiple items need a packing plan.
          </h2>

          <p>
            This calculator does not arrange several
            items, choose among multiple cartons or
            calculate protective spacing. For a real
            multi-item problem, open the
            {' '}
            <a href="/personal/">
              Personal workspace
            </a>
            {' '}
            or use the
            {' '}
            <a href="/business/">
              Business workspace
            </a>
            {' '}
            for inventory, cost and weight inputs.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
            Internal vs external dimensions
          </p>

          <h2>
            Packing fit uses the inside of the box.
          </h2>

          <p>
            This tool compares the item against
            internal box dimensions because those
            describe usable packing space. External
            dimensions serve a different purpose,
            including dimensional-weight analysis.
            Use the
            {' '}
            <a href="/tools/dimensional-weight-calculator/">
              dimensional-weight calculator
            </a>
            {' '}
            when you need that shipping-volume
            calculation.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
